You are right, and the imbalance was worse than the section you commented on: the introduction had the same one-sided framing. It ended with "an upgrade of the Toolkit never changes a component you already installed", which reads as reassurance while describing exactly the drawback you are pointing at.

Both are rewritten. The section is now named "The Component Is Yours, For Better and For Worse" and says the two halves plainly:

- files are yours to edit, without an override mechanism and without being limited to the options the author thought of;
- there is no upgrade path. Fixes, new options and new features added to a kit never reach a component already installed. Reinstalling copies the current files, which may differ both from what you started with and from what you have since edited, so bringing an improvement in means comparing and merging by hand. The command asks before overwriting, so nothing is lost, but the merge is yours to do.
- and the consequence across projects: installing a component today and in six months gives two different versions, and they do not drift back together.

The introduction now points forward to that section instead of selling one half of the trade, since it is the thing to understand before adopting the Toolkit rather than a detail near the end.

